<div class="sop-meta">
  <div><strong>Trigger</strong> Quarterly review of local network coverage; ad-hoc when invited to an event</div>
  <div><strong>Owner</strong> Jon</div>
  <div><strong>Time</strong> 4-6 hours/month consistent presence</div>
  <div><strong>Output</strong> ~6 active relationships maintained, 1-2 referrals per quarter</div>
</div>

<h2>Where to show up</h2>

<h3>Tier 1: must-be-active in</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Local Chamber of Commerce.</strong> Annual membership. Attend monthly mixer + one committee. Speak at one event per year (offer a free site audit to the membership as the hook).</li>
  <li><strong>One BNI / business networking group.</strong> Visiting member, then full member if the chemistry works after 3 visits. The "one referral expected per month" structure is the value.</li>
  <li><strong>One small-business association.</strong> SBA SCORE chapter, NFIB, or local equivalent. Volunteer 2 hours/month mentoring; the relationships build naturally.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Tier 2: trade-specific, depending on focus</h3>
<ul>
  <li>NRCA (roofers), ACCA (HVAC), PHCC (plumbers), NECA (electricians), NALP (landscapers), InterNACHI / ASHI (inspectors). Affiliate-adjacent membership where available; speak at the local chapter where I have content to share.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Tier 3: business-coach + adjacent-service ecosystem</h3>
<p>The single highest-yield relationship type for a sole-prop agency. Local business coaches, EOS implementers, fractional CMOs, accountants who specialize in trades, lawyers who do small-business contracts. Their clients ARE my prospect list, and they are constantly asked "do you know a good web person?".</p>
<ul>
  <li>Identify 8-10 in the metro.</li>
  <li>Coffee with each one over the first 90 days.</li>
  <li>Send each a relevant blog post or audit insight every 60-90 days.</li>
  <li>Refer them business in return when the opportunity comes up.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Cadence</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Weekly:</strong> 1 in-person event or coffee. Can be Tier 1, 2, or 3.</li>
  <li><strong>Monthly:</strong> Touch base with each Tier 3 contact. One-line email or text, no ask. "Saw this and thought of you" energy.</li>
  <li><strong>Quarterly:</strong> Audit the network. Are the Tier 3 relationships warm? Have I given more than I have asked? Drop the ones that are not reciprocating.</li>
  <li><strong>Annually:</strong> Speak at chamber + 1 trade association. Concrete value-give to the room.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Steps for a new Tier 3 introduction</h2>
<ul class="sop-checklist">
  <li>LinkedIn DM or warm intro email. Lead with a specific reason ("you wrote a post about X, here is one observation that builds on it").</li>
  <li>Coffee meeting. Listen 70%; ask "who is the right kind of client for you?" and "what is a referral that landed well recently?".</li>
  <li>Within 48 hours: send a follow-up note plus one piece of value (a blog post, an audit observation, an introduction).</li>
  <li>Add to the quarterly check-in list. Tag in CRM: source, business focus, last touch.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common issues</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Going to events but no follow-up.</strong> The networking value is in the follow-up, not the event. If after-event note is not sent within 48 hours, the contact is lost.</li>
  <li><strong>Asking too soon.</strong> Tier 3 relationships compound slowly. The first ask is no earlier than month 3 of the relationship. Earlier asks burn the contact.</li>
  <li><strong>Quantity over quality.</strong> Five strong Tier 3 contacts beat thirty weak ones. Pick well.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Exit criteria</h2>
<ul>
  <li>~6 actively warm Tier 3 contacts maintained at any time.</li>
  <li>1-2 referrals per quarter sourced from the network.</li>
  <li>Annual chamber speaking slot scheduled (or one trade-association equivalent).</li>
</ul>
